CRITICAL RULES:
1. NEVER fabricate achievements — every bullet must trace back to the master CV
2. NEVER emit bracketed placeholders or LaTeX markup — if a value is unknown, omit the clause entirely; write £ for GBP, never \textsterling or $...$ notation
3. You may rephrase, reorder, and emphasise — but not invent
4. Embed ATS keywords naturally — not awkwardly stuffed
5. Preserve the master CV's overall length (target within 10%), every role, and the same number of achievement bullets per role
6. Reword bullets from the master CV; if a bullet has no metric, do NOT invent one — rephrase without a number
7. Use the contract_type to frame the candidate correctly: "permanent" roles need employee framing, not "contractor"; "contract" roles should reference delivery tenure and daily/hourly rate context
8. Unsupported JD keywords are honest gaps. Do not insert them; the user can accept those missing keywords during review
9. Copy role, company, period, and certification values verbatim. Never turn an employer, client, award, or achievement into a certification
10. Preserve education exactly as supplied in the master CV. Do not rewrite qualification names, institutions, dates, grades, or fields

ANTI-EXAMPLE — do not do this:
  Master: "Improved deployment process at AcmeCo, saving time"
  BAD tailored: "Reduced deployment time by 40% at AcmeCo, achieving 99.9% uptime" ← invented metric, never do this
  GOOD tailored: "Streamlined deployment pipeline at AcmeCo, accelerating release cadence" ← rephrased without inventing a number

SECTOR TRANSFER GUIDANCE:
If the candidate's experience is in different sectors than the target (e.g. energy/aviation vs construction), do NOT claim sector-specific experience they don't have. Instead, surface transferable proof points: regulated-industry delivery, large-programme governance, data-platform consolidation, stakeholder management at scale. Frame these in the target sector's language.

SKILL CATEGORY LABELS:
Use the candidate's existing skill group names from the master CV. Do not introduce new category names that do not appear in the master CV. Only include a category if the candidate genuinely has skills for it.

